research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-49-{"id":"ex-polynomial-integrals-by-the-ftc","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~demmel/ma128a_Spr02/LectureNotes/Integration.html"],"rationale":"The university notes state exactly integral_0^1 x^m dx=1/(m+1) for nonnegative integer m. The item translates the integer into the library's canonical real embedding and supplies local FTC and consistency checks.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-50-{"id":"ex-additivity-on-a-step-function","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"The particular two-level step function and its paired verification routes are a local worked construction. Splitting at 1 gives two constant integrals 1 and 3, and the displayed three-point Darboux partition gives matching sums after the single jump is isolated; the value 4 is directly verified.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-51-{"id":"ex-integral-test-applied-to-the-p-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_series.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_impropriemann.html"],"rationale":"The sources give the p-series threshold p>1 and the integral test. The item specializes to rational powers available in the library, indexes f_p(t)=(t+1)^{-p} from zero, and expresses improper convergence as boundedness of its proper-integral sequence. The mathematical threshold is sourced, but its last p=1 sentence falsely denies existence of a primitive and is proposed for repair.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-52-{"id":"ex-two-root-x-and-its-unbounded-derivative","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://clp.math.uky.edu/clp1/sec_def_deriv.html","https://faculty.gvsu.edu/boelkinm/Home/ACS/sec-6-5-improper.html"],"rationale":"The CLP university text computes (sqrt x)'=1/(2sqrt x) for x>0 and states that the derivative is undefined at zero; Active Calculus treats 1/sqrt x as an unbounded integrand at zero. The item scales by 2, proves the precise unbounded sequence, and packages those facts to explain why the proper Riemann FTC does not apply on [0,1].","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-10"}
